  "text": "Affix - A morpheme that is attached to another morpheme to modify its meaning, such as prefixes, suffixes, and infixes.\n\nAllophone - A variant form of a phoneme that occurs in a specific phonetic environment without changing the meaning of a word.\n\nAllophony - The variation in pronunciation of a phoneme depending on its phonetic context.\n\nAllophonic variation - The phenomenon where a phoneme can have different allophones in different phonetic environments.\n\nAssimilation - A phonological process where a sound becomes more like a neighboring sound, either in pronunciation or in articulation.\n\nBilingualism - The ability to speak and understand two or more languages fluently.\n\nCase - A grammatical category that indicates the role of a noun in a sentence, such as subject, object, or possessor.\n\nChomsky - A reference to Noam Chomsky, a prominent linguist known for his work on transformational grammar and the theory of universal grammar.\n\nClosure - The process in speech production where the articulators come together to form a stop consonant.\n\nConsonant - A speech sound produced by obstructing the airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nConsonant cluster - A sequence of two or more consonants that occur together without a vowel in between.\n\nConsonant phoneme - A distinct unit of sound in a language that is a consonant.\n\nConsonantal - Relating to consonants, or a speech sound that is produced by obstructing the airflow.\n\nConsonant cluster - A sequence of consonants that occur together without a vowel.\n\nConstruct - A mental model or conceptual framework used in the analysis of linguistic phenomena.\n\nDichotomy - A division into two distinct parts or categories.\n\nDiphthong - A vowel sound that is produced by a glide from one vowel to another within the same syllable.\n\nDissimilation - A phonological process where a sound becomes less like a neighboring sound.\n\nDistributed morphology - An approach to morphology that treats morphological processes as operations on a representation rather than as rules.\n\nDative - A grammatical case used to indicate the indirect object of a verb.\n\nDative shift - A syntactic phenomenon where the object of a verb is moved to a prepositional phrase.\n\nDemeaning - A sociolinguistic term referring to negative attitudes or judgments about certain language varieties or speakers.\n\nDeletion - The loss of a sound or word in speech, often due to articulatory ease or phonological rules.\n\nDialect - A variety of a language distinguished by vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation.\n\nDiphthong - A vowel sound that involves a glide from one vowel to another within the same syllable.\n\nDiphthongization - The process by which a vowel becomes a diphthong.\n\nDislocation - The movement of a constituent from its normal position in a sentence.\n\nDissimilation - A phonological process where a sound becomes less like a neighboring sound.\n\nDistributivity - A property of linguistic features where a sound or word appears in certain environments but not others.\n\nDistributive - Pertaining to distribution, such as the distribution of phonemes or morphemes.\n\nDivergence - A linguistic phenomenon where two language varieties become more different over time.\n\nDivergent - Relating to divergence, such as the divergence of languages or dialects.\n\nD vowel - A vowel in English that is pronounced with the tongue in a central position, such as the \"a\" in \"father.\"\n\nEjective - A type of consonant produced by a burst of air from the buildup of pressure in the vocal tract.\n\nEjective consonant - A consonant produced by a sudden release of compressed air.\n\nElongation - The lengthening of a vowel sound, often used to convey emphasis or emotion.\n\nEpicene",
